1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,080
Each map holds five hundred thirty-seven bomb hits.

2
00:00:03,460 --> 00:00:06,300
On one, every hit landed by pure chance.

3
00:00:06,680 --> 00:00:08,230
The other follows a rule.

4
00:00:08,610 --> 00:00:09,910
Which one is chance?

5
00:00:11,766 --> 00:00:13,206
Both are simulated.

6
00:00:13,586 --> 00:00:14,856
The question isn't.

7
00:00:15,236 --> 00:00:23,636
In nineteen forty-four, Londoners claimed German flying bombs fell in clusters, and precise aim would put factories at risk.

8
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:26,270
Build the detector your eye is running.

9
00:00:26,650 --> 00:00:34,420
Cut each map into five hundred seventy-six squares, count the hits, and flag any square with three or more, like this one.

10
00:00:34,766 --> 00:00:37,206
Map A lights up with thirty-nine targets.

11
00:00:37,586 --> 00:00:38,846
Map B gets none.

12
00:00:39,226 --> 00:00:40,486
So A looks aimed.

13
00:00:40,833 --> 00:00:41,733
Backwards.

14
00:00:42,633 --> 00:00:48,283
I dropped every hit on map A at random, so all thirty-nine targets are false alarms.

15
00:00:48,663 --> 00:00:52,723
Map B's rule: no two hits closer than four fifths of a square.

16
00:00:53,103 --> 00:00:57,653
The detector, like your eye, expected chance to spread out that evenly.

17
00:00:58,000 --> 00:00:59,110
Take one square.

18
00:00:59,490 --> 00:01:03,870
Each bomb has a one in five hundred seventy-six chance of landing in it.

19
00:01:04,250 --> 00:01:07,820
To stay empty, all five hundred thirty-seven must miss.

20
00:01:08,166 --> 00:01:10,266
Guess how many squares get no hit at all.

21
00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:14,950
About two hundred twenty-seven squares stay empty.

22
00:01:15,330 --> 00:01:17,230
Map A has two hundred twenty-two.

23
00:01:17,566 --> 00:01:27,336
Those empty squares force the clumps: the other three hundred fifty-four squares share all the hits, one and a half each, so plenty must double up.

24
00:01:27,716 --> 00:01:30,916
To judge London, chance must predict every bar.

25
00:01:31,266 --> 00:01:33,496
Call the average hits per square lambda.

26
00:01:33,996 --> 00:01:40,516
One minus a small slice, raised to a big power, is almost exactly e to the minus lambda.

27
00:01:41,366 --> 00:01:50,516
For two hits, pick which two bombs land here: five thirty-seven times five thirty-six ways, over two, since order doesn't matter.

28
00:01:50,916 --> 00:01:57,626
Each lands with chance one in five seventy-six, and the rest miss: e to the minus lambda again.

29
00:01:58,366 --> 00:02:01,546
Five thirty-six is almost five thirty-seven.

30
00:02:01,946 --> 00:02:06,336
For three hits, each two becomes a three; for any k, a k.

31
00:02:06,936 --> 00:02:11,786
The powers merge into lambda to the k: the Poisson distribution.

32
00:02:12,486 --> 00:02:17,656
Times five hundred seventy-six squares, it predicts these purple bars.

33
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,950
The honest detector compares whole histograms.

34
00:02:21,750 --> 00:02:24,830
Thirty-nine targets, and chance predicts thirty-nine.

35
00:02:25,210 --> 00:02:35,660
The chi-squared misfit: square each bar's gap and divide by chance's bar, so a miss of two on seven outweighs a miss of five on two hundred twenty-seven.

36
00:02:36,040 --> 00:02:38,680
Add them up: map A scores two point oh.

37
00:02:39,060 --> 00:02:42,060
Three in four simulated chance maps score worse.

38
00:02:42,360 --> 00:02:44,790
That share, p, is point seven four.

39
00:02:45,133 --> 00:02:52,343
Map B: half the expected empty squares, a tower of single hits, and a misfit of two hundred seventy-two.

40
00:02:52,723 --> 00:02:56,223
A pattern this even is the fingerprint of a spacing rule.

41
00:02:56,566 --> 00:03:01,516
Aimed bombs would miss the other way: extra empty squares, extra pile-ups.

42
00:03:01,896 --> 00:03:13,646
In nineteen forty-six, the actuary R D Clarke published this test on real flying-bomb hits: five hundred thirty-seven, in five hundred seventy-six squares of London.

43
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:19,690
Chance predicted two hundred twenty-seven empty squares; Clarke counted two hundred twenty-nine.

44
00:03:19,890 --> 00:03:23,730
Single hits: two hundred eleven, exactly as predicted.

45
00:03:23,930 --> 00:03:26,430
Same test: p equals point eight eight.

46
00:03:26,766 --> 00:03:34,436
The bombs were aimed at Tower Bridge, but half fell more than twelve kilometres away, outside this circle and off this map.

47
00:03:34,816 --> 00:03:43,456
And the test needs one lambda everywhere: farther north, hits were about half as dense, so Clarke chose an area where lambda held steady.

48
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:44,990
So look again.

49
00:03:46,190 --> 00:03:50,000
The clumps in map A are what no aim at all looks like.

50
00:03:50,380 --> 00:03:54,510
If you chose map B, you chose the one following a rule.

